# Exam track — Describing a Picture: Living Room — Sub-lesson 3: Weather Description
#
# The WÉINI task for the living-room photo of sub-lessons 1–2: the time of day,
# the season, and the weather. This is the one place in the theme where the
# candidate is allowed to *infer* rather than report, so the point of the file is
# the hedging register: `ech mengen`, `villäicht`, `warscheinlech`,
# `Ech géif soen`, `Et kéint … sinn`, `Ech sinn net sécher, mee …`.
#
# The photo makes that unavoidable, and that is why this photo is good for the
# task: the family is INDOORS, so the weather is not visible at all. The honest
# answer is "I can't see the weather, maybe it is cold" — a real Sproochentest
# answer, and a better lesson than pretending to read a sky. What evidence there
# is: daylight and greenery through the window on the left, lamps and a candle
# lit, everyone in long sleeves and a jumper, hot coffee.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 and 02 — no @question prompts, no first-person
# opinion. A hedged inference *about the photo* ("it is probably autumn, because
# everyone is wearing warm clothes") is not an opinion: it is an observation with
# a confidence marker, and a stranger can still check the evidence against the
# picture.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses or short two-clause sentences, no attributive adjectives on masculine or
# neuter nouns — which is why there is no `en waarmen Owend` here.
#
# Words sub-lessons 1–2 already teach (d'Bild, d'Stuff, d'Leit, gesinn, d'Fënster
# is NOT taught in 01 either, …) are deliberately not repeated — the pass-gate
# makes them prerequisites and stat keys are shared app-wide. The window belongs
# to this file rather than 01, because it is this file's only evidence.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry the highest-value frames in the theme, because a
# hedge is a *pattern* that transfers to every picture and every topic: the
# `Ech mengen, …` opener, the `well`-clause that justifies a guess (verb-final —
# read, not assembled), and the "I cannot see it, because they are inside" frame
# that this photo exists to teach.

@lesson L1.03 "Living Room: Weather Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# it during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it describes
# the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/living-room/img/living-room.webp"
@image-alt "The same living-room photo, seen for the time and the weather: daylight and green leaves through the window on the left, lamps and a candle lit, everyone in long sleeves drinking hot coffee — the weather itself is not visible, because the family is indoors."

# --- Weather and season ---
@word d'Wieder = the weather
@word d'Joreszäit = the season
@word de Summer = the summer
@word den Hierscht = the autumn
@word de Wanter = the winter
@word de Reen = the rain
@word kal = cold
@word waarm = warm
@word reenen = to rain

# --- Evidence in the room ---
@word d'Fënster = the window
@word d'Blieder = the leaves
@word d'Luuchten = the lamps
@word d'Käerz = the candle
@word d'Kleedung = the clothing
@word dobannen = indoors
@word dobaussen = outdoors
@word duerch = through
@word hell = bright

# --- Time ---
@word den Owend = the evening
@word de Nomëtteg = the afternoon
@word d'Woch = the week
@word de Sonndeg = the Sunday
@word owes = in the evening
@word nomëttes = in the afternoon

# --- Hedging a guess ---
@word mengen = to think
@word warscheinlech = probably
@word villäicht = maybe
@word sécher = sure
@word well = because

@sentence
@lu D'Famill ass dobannen an ech gesinn d'Wieder net.
@en The family is indoors and I do not see the weather.
@distractor-lu Joreszäit
@distractor-lu Fënster
@distractor-en season
@distractor-en window

@sentence
@lu Duerch d'Fënster gesinn ech gréng Blieder.
@en I see green leaves through the window.
@distractor-lu Luucht
@distractor-lu Käerz
@distractor-en lamp
@distractor-en candle

@sentence
@lu Villäicht ass et kal dobaussen.
@en Maybe it is cold outside.
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu Reen
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en rain

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht reent et.
@en I am not sure, but maybe it is raining.
@distractor-lu warscheinlech
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en probably
@distractor-en weather

@sentence
@lu D'Leit hu waarm Kleedung un.
@en The people are wearing warm clothing.
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-lu Blieder
@distractor-en cold
@distractor-en leaves

@sentence
@lu D'Joreszäit ass warscheinlech den Hierscht.
@en The season is probably autumn.
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en weather

@sentence
@lu Et ass hell dobaussen an net Owend.
@en It is bright outside and not evening.
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-lu Käerz
@distractor-en cold
@distractor-en candle

@sentence
@lu D'Luuchten an eng Käerz sinn un.
@en The lamps and a candle are on.
@distractor-lu Fënster
@distractor-lu Kleedung
@distractor-en window
@distractor-en clothing

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, et ass e Sonndeg nomëttes.
@en I think it is a Sunday afternoon.
@distractor-lu owes
@distractor-lu Woch
@distractor-en evening
@distractor-en week

@sentence
@lu Et kéint och en Owend an der Woch sinn.
@en It could also be an evening during the week.
@distractor-lu Nomëtteg
@distractor-lu Sonndeg
@distractor-en afternoon
@distractor-en Sunday

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The hedge opener — the most transferable frame in the exam track: `Ech mengen,
# …` prefixes any guess, on any picture or topic. The comma marks the clause
# boundary and exempts the blank from the Eifeler Regel. Season nouns are the
# blank; both season distractors are contradicted by the warm clothing, and the
# third is a noun of a different kind entirely.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [Hierscht].
@en I think it is [autumn].
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en weather

# The justification frame — the shape that turns a guess into an exam-worthy
# answer. `well` sends the verb to the end (`unhuet` last), so the learner reads
# that order rather than assembling it.
#
# Distractor choice is load-bearing: `sécher` would be grammatical AND coherent
# in a hedge slot, which makes it a second correct answer, not a wrong one — the
# trap recorded in the memory file. So the distractors are `net`, which
# contradicts the reason offered by the `well`-clause, plus two nouns, which
# cannot occupy an adverb slot before a bare noun at all.
@fill
@lu Et ass [warscheinlech] Hierscht, well d'Leit waarm Kleedung unhunn.
@en It is [probably] autumn, because the people are wearing warm clothing.
@distractor-lu net
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-en not
@distractor-en summer
@distractor-en weather

# The frame this photo exists to teach: the candidate cannot see the weather, and
# saying so with a reason is a full answer rather than a failure. Two blanks in
# different clauses AND different word classes (noun, then adjective), so they
# cannot swap (R6). `well si an der Stuff sinn` is verb-final and read, not built.
#
# `waarm` was authored here as a distractor and the builder audit killed it: in
# the second blank it is grammatical AND coherent — the frame's own first clause
# says the weather is not visible, so "maybe it is warm" is a second correct
# answer, not a wrong one. Exactly the `sécher`-in-a-hedge-slot trap recorded in
# .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md. Every distractor here is a noun
# instead: impossible in the adjective blank, and false about the photo in the
# noun blank (the window, the leaves and the candle are all plainly visible).
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn d'[Wieder] net, well si an der Stuff sinn, villäicht ass et [kal].
@en I do not see the [weather], because they are in the living room, maybe it is [cold].
@distractor-lu Fënster
@distractor-lu Käerz
@distractor-lu Blieder
@distractor-en window
@distractor-en candle
@distractor-en leaves
